  :adr/body "# ADR 2799008182: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8182 Steam Engine and Boiler Operators boiler-plant scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8182\n-- Steam Engine and Boiler Operators** only, Wave3 (production/trades)\nof the rollout: Wave0/1/2 100% complete, Wave3 batches 1-14 already\nlanded 84 occupations prior to this batch, and this ADR is part of a\nbatch pushing to close the remaining Wave3 gaps.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8182\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone (`git clone --depth 1` of both\n`kotoba-lang/occupation` and its `../technology` sibling dependency)\nbefore any work began, via\n`clojure -M -e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println\n(kotoba.occupation/get-occupation \\\"8182\\\"))\"`, which returned `{:id\n8182, :name Steam Engine and Boiler Operators, :maturity :spec,\n:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger],\n:optional-technologies [], :operating-states [:intake :propose\n:approve :execute :audit]}` -- confirming the `:name` reads exactly\n\"Steam Engine and Boiler Operators\", no mismatch. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec`, with no `:repo`/`:business-id`;\nno repository existed at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8182` (`gh\napi` 404 confirmed before scaffolding). This ADR and its companion\nregistry edit promote the `8182` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nThis session's reference repo,\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8131`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8131)\n(Chemical Products Plant and Machine Operators), was confirmed live\nand `:implemented`, cloned and read in full -- its `store`/`advisor`/\n`governor`/`actor` split, full doc set, and closed-allowlist/scope-\nexclusion pattern for a comparable high-hazard industrial-process\npattern were mirrored closely, adapting the exclusion language from\nchemical-process terms to boiler/pressure/valve terms as instructed.\nThe `langgraph.graph` pin (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`\n`:git/sha \"5b4e5e70837b849b14b6a5800894e3be202ad5fa\"`) was verified\nidentical against the most recently created sibling repo in this same\nbatch, `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8181` (Glass and Ceramics\nPlant Operators), before use.\n\n**This actor carries a high-pressure boiler-explosion life-safety-\ncritical hazard framing, comparable to the mining (ISCO 8111) and\nchemical-plant (ISCO 8131) hazard framings already established in\nthis catalog.** Steam Engine and Boiler Operators run high-pressure\nsteam/boiler systems -- catastrophic explosion risk if pressure or\nsafety-valve controls fail, comparable in stakes to the already-landed\nchemical-plant and mining actors. The closed op allowlist therefore\nmust NEVER include any op that directly finalizes a boiler-operation-\nexecution decision (authorizing pressure changes/valve operations to\nproceed) or a plant-safety-clearance decision, nor override a plant\nsafety officer's judgment -- these are always a hard, permanent,\nstructural block (absent from the allowlist entirely, not merely\ngated), never auto-commit-eligible under any confidence level. Any\nsafety/anomaly flag (pressure-reading concern, safety-valve condition,\nequipment-condition issue) ALWAYS escalates, no exceptions, ever.\n\n**This actor coordinates PLANT SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY -- it never\noperates boiler/steam equipment itself and never makes a boiler-\noperation-execution or plant-safety-clearance decision.** It is not an\nauthority to decide when or whether a pressure change or valve\noperation proceeds; it is a coordination layer for pressure-log/\nmaintenance-record/progress record logging, crew/shift scheduling,\nsafety-concern surfacing and administrative/spare-parts supply order\ncoordination (never pressure vessels, valves or steam equipment\nthemselves -- boiler/steam equipment handling is entirely out of\nscope for this administrative-coordination actor), all gated behind\nan independent governor. It never finalizes a boiler-operation-\nexecution decision and never performs or completes a boiler operation.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8182` as an OSS\ndocumentation/coordination actor for steam engine and boiler\noperators, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Boiler-Plant\nScheduling & Logistics Coordination Advisor ⊣ BoilerCoordGovernor** on\na `langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`,\ngit-sha-pinned identically to the reference's `deps.edn` and to the\nmost recently landed sibling repo in this batch -- a different library\ncoordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated\n`20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor, human-in-\nthe-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit\nledger. All source is portable `.cljc` (no JVM interop); tests are\n`clojure.test` `.clj`, matching the reference repo's own layout.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- pressure-log/maintenance-record/progress data\n  logging (administrative metadata only)\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/shift/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a pressure-reading/safety-valve-\n  condition/equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible, no confidence-level exception, ever\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- administrative/spare-parts procurement\n  proposal (NOT pressure vessels, valves or steam equipment themselves\n  -- boiler/steam equipment handling is entirely out of scope for this\n  administrative-coordination actor), escalates above a registered\n  cost threshold\n\nNo op in this allowlist finalizes a boiler-operation-execution\ndecision (authorizing a pressure change/valve operation to proceed),\nfinalizes a plant-safety-clearance decision, or overrides a plant\nsafety officer's judgment -- these decision classes are structurally\nabsent from the allowlist, not merely gated behind escalation.\n\n### Six HARD governor checks (`boilercoord.governor`, permanent, never\noverridable by human approval)\n\n1. **no-plant** -- the operator/plant record must be independently\n   verified/registered before any action.\n2. **no-actuation** -- proposal `:effect` must be `:propose` (the\n   governor never dispatches hardware and never operates boiler/steam\n   equipment).\n3. **unknown-op** -- `:op` must be one of the four allowlisted\n   coordination ops; the closed op-allowlist is enforced\n   unconditionally.\n4. **plant-mismatch** -- if the proposal names a plant, it must be the\n   SAME plant verified for this request (defense-in-depth against a\n   proposal quietly targeting an unverified plant).\n5. **unknown-operator / operator-wrong-plant** -- if the proposal\n   references an operator, that operator must be a REGISTERED\n   certified steam-engine/boiler operator belonging to this plant.\n6. **scope-exclusion-violation** -- a proposal that attempts, in any\n   free-text field, to authorize a pressure change/valve operation to\n   proceed, to finalize a plant-safety-clearance decision, or to\n   override a plant safety officer's judgment is a HARD, PERMANENT\n   block, evaluated unconditionally on content, independent of `:op`.\n\n### Escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (`boilercoord.governor/always-escalate-ops`). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a pressure-reading/\n  safety-valve-condition/equipment-condition concern -- it never\n  resolves the concern itself, and this is unconditional: no\n  confidence level, however high, ever exempts a safety flag from\n  human sign-off.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-order-cost-threshold` (20000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-or-below-cost-threshold`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence.\n- advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` (0.6) also escalates,\n  for any op.\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare nouns (e.g.\n\"boiler\", \"steam\", \"pressure\", \"valve\") can accidentally match inside\nthe mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate\nproposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this risk is\nespecially sharp here because this actor's ENTIRE domain is boiler-\nplant coordination, so bare nouns like \"boiler\"/\"steam\"/\"pressure\"/\n\"valve\" are ordinary, unavoidable vocabulary.\n`boilercoord.governor/scope-exclusion-phrases` is phrased exclusively\nas finalization/execution ACTION PHRASES (e.g. \"authorize the\npressure-valve operation to proceed\", \"finalize the boiler-operation\ndecision\", \"override the plant safety officer's judgment\"), never\nbare nouns, and\n`boilercoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor (including a\n`:flag-safety-concern` request whose description names an \"unresolved\npressure-reading concern near unit 3 boiler, equipment-condition\nreview pending\"), never trips the scope-exclusion guard. A companion\ntest, `scope-exclusion-not-triggered-by-bare-domain-nouns`, asserts\ndirectly against `governor/scope-exclusion-violation?` that a\nrationale/description containing only bare domain nouns (\"proposed\nschedule-crew-operation for boiler unit 3 steam-system preparation on\nplant BP-1\", \"operator fully certified for pressure-vessel handling\nand valve procedure documentation for this boiler\") does not trip the\nguard, while\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-authorize-pressure-valve-operation-to-proceed`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-boiler-operation-decision`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-plant-safety-clearance`\nand\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-override-plant-safety-officer`\nconfirm the actual forbidden action phrases DO trip it, and\n`holds-a-scope-excluded-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`,\n`holds-a-finalize-boiler-operation-decision-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`\nand\n`holds-an-override-plant-safety-officer-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`\nconfirm the full actor graph resolves such attempts straight to\n`:hold` (a finish point, never `:request-approval`) with no record\ncommitted.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8182\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8182` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8182`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry, not\n  assumed, immediately before the edit to account for concurrent\n  sibling landings in this same batch -- six sibling agents were\n  landing occupations concurrently).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 26 tests, 66 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a fresh clone of the pushed repository (mojibake scan: 0\n  replacement characters found).\n- `clojure -M:lint` (clj-kondo): 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`) -- a single-entity EDN vector with an\n  `:adr/body` string blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-8131`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n- No op that finalizes a boiler-operation-execution decision,\n  finalizes a plant-safety-clearance decision, or overrides a plant\n  safety officer's judgment exists anywhere in this actor's code --\n  this is a structural absence (closed allowlist), not a runtime-gated\n  exclusion, and is the load-bearing safety property this ADR records.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-boiler-operation-authorization`\n  or `:authorize-pressure-change` that is merely gated behind\n  `always-escalate-ops`: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  boiler-safety guidance -- an always-escalate op still means the\n  *capability* to finalize a boiler-operation-execution decision\n  exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For\n  a decision that can directly cause a catastrophic pressure-vessel/\n  boiler explosion from a pressure- or safety-valve-control failure,\n  the capability itself must not exist in the allowlist at all -- this\n  matches the strict bar already established by\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8131` (chemical-process-authorization decisions)\n  and the registry's mining framing (ISCO 8111).\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"boiler\" \"steam\"\n  \"pressure\" \"valve\"]`): rejected per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class -- this actor's entire domain is boiler-plant coordination, so\n  bare nouns would self-trip on essentially every legitimate proposal\n  (work-record logs naming \"boiler unit 3 progress notes\", crew-\n  schedule proposals naming \"boiler shift changeover\", safety flags\n  naming \"pressure-reading concern\", supply orders naming \"boiler-\n  plant PPE and administrative unit-3 spare-parts signage\", etc.).\n  Scope-exclusion terms were phrased exclusively as multi-word\n  finalization/execution ACTION phrases instead, per the task's\n  explicit self-tripping-bug-pattern guidance, and a dedicated\n  regression test asserts the default mock advisor's proposals across\n  every allowlisted op never self-trip.\n- Checking plant/operator provenance only from the proposal (never the\n  request): rejected -- the plant-mismatch check requires the plant\n  identity to be anchored at the request level and independently\n  cross-checked against anything the proposal claims, so a proposal\n  that quietly names a different, unverified plant is caught even if\n  the proposal otherwise looks well-formed.\n- Modeling the operator-registration check as unconditionally required\n  on every op: rejected as too rigid -- `:coordinate-supply-order`\n  legitimately has no operator to reference (it is a materials-\n  procurement proposal against the plant, not a person); the check\n  applies only when a proposal actually references an `:operator-id`.\n- Allowing `:coordinate-supply-order` to cover pressure vessels, valves\n  or steam equipment: rejected outright per the task's explicit scope\n  guidance -- this actor coordinates PLANT SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY,\n  and pressure-vessel/valve/steam-equipment handling is entirely out\n  of scope; the op's docstring and every fixture in this repo name\n  only plant-equipment/administrative/spare-parts items (boiler-plant\n  PPE, procurement signage), never pressure vessels, valves or steam\n  equipment themselves.\n"}]
